 \subsection{Policy implications for Bank Indonesia, OJK, and CFX}
-Three concrete regulatory insights emerge. First, policymakers evaluating onshore crypto market efficiency should monitor the domestic \texttt{USDT/IDR}--JISDOR basis rather than unadjusted \texttt{BTC/IDR} premia, because domestic stablecoin conversion accounts for the bulk of total basis variation. Second, CFX and KKI's two-stage $50\%$ fee reductions in March and October 2026 compressed statutory domestic friction by $3.33\text{ bps}$ round-trip on IDR pairs ($65.44\text{ bps} \rightarrow 62.11\text{ bps}$ taker), whereas PMK 50/2025 ($0\%$ buyer VAT, $21\text{ bps}$ seller PPh 22) shifted statutory tax incidence onto sellers without altering symmetric round-trip statutory costs. Third, taxing onshore sell transactions at $21\text{ bps}$ alongside a $100\text{ bps}$ statutory rate on offshore venues creates a substantial statutory wedge favoring onshore execution, although quantifying actual onshore-versus-offshore order-flow migration requires venue-level volume data beyond our price-based dataset.
